abstract: >-
  This work presents an end-to-end pipeline for spatio-temporal
  crop health assessment in the Jammu region of India. Multi-
  source Earth observation data — Sentinel-2 surface reflectance,
  MODIS land-surface temperature, and CHIRPS precipitation — are
  harmonised over 150 fixed sampling points to derive seven
  vegetation and hydro-climatic features. A composite Crop Health
  Score is modelled with classical and deep-learning regressors
  under a strict temporal hold-out, interpreted via SHAP, and
  extended to recursive multi-step forecasting. A Streamlit
  application exposes the trained model for on-demand,
  coordinate-level prediction.
keywords:
  - remote sensing
  - precision agriculture
  - crop health
  - Sentinel-2
  - MODIS
  - CHIRPS
  - Google Earth Engine
  - machine learning
  - time series forecasting
  - Jammu and Kashmir
